.TH FLEET-OWNERS 1 "2026-11-05" "cti-agent 1.5" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
fleet-owners \- who owns each web application: owners.db, and the snapshot the lanes read
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo cti\-agent fleet\-owners
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fleet-owners
keeps the application owners, watchers, staging links
.RB ( covers ),
websites with no scan yet, and the domains an owner or watcher may be at, in
the SQLite database
.IR /etc/cti\-agent/owners.db ,
with a history of every change. It replaces hand-editing
.IR app\-owners.txt .
.PP
After every change it rewrites
.IR /etc/cti\-agent/owners.json ,
atomically and from the same transaction. That snapshot is what
.BR cti\-appscan (1)
and
.B cti\-mailer \-\-lane owner
read: Go's standard library has no SQLite driver and the Go module takes no
dependencies, so the Go lanes never open the database. While there is no
snapshot they read
.IR app\-owners.txt ;
once there is one they read only the snapshot, and an unreadable snapshot
stops the owners lane rather than falling back to the file.
.PP
It runs as
.BR root ,
through the wrapper, and is the one fleet command that does.
.I owners.db
is root:root 0600 and
.I owners.json
root:ctiagent 0640, so the service account can read who receives reports and
cannot change it. The change history records
.B SUDO_USER
and cannot be edited or deleted.
.PP
.I APP
is the scanner's web app id,
.BI id: N
(it survives a rename; use it), or the application's exact name. Entries
follow the same rules as
.I app\-owners.txt
lines: one owner email, at most 10 watchers, no placeholder address.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Create an empty
.I owners.db
and snapshot. Does nothing if the database exists.
.TP
.BI import " FILE"
Load an
.I app\-owners.txt
into an empty database, reporting each line it could not use.
.I FILE
must be an absolute path to a regular file owned by root, writable by
nobody else, in a directory with the same rule. A placeholder
line with
.B covers=
is kept for its covers only.
.TP
.B \-\-replace
With
.BR import :
replace the applications and sites already in the database.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow\-domains " LIST"
With
.BR import :
also allow these comma-separated domains, typically the value of
.BR OWNER_ALLOW_DOMAINS .
.TP
.B list
Every application and site with its scanner name, owner, component, watcher
count and covers. An id with no stored name says so; see
.BR names .
.TP
.BI names " FILE"
Store the scanner's application names, from a listing written by
.B cti\-agent cti\-appscan \-\-list\-apps
(the text after the
.B #
on each line). Names are for people: they appear in
.B list
and
.BR show ,
are never written to
.IR owners.json ,
and decide nothing. Only applications already in the database are named; the
rest are counted. Run it after the scanner gains or renames an application.
.I FILE
follows the same trust rules as
.BR import :
an absolute path, a regular file owned by root and writable by nobody else, so
write it as root, for example
.BR "sudo sh \-c 'cti\-agent cti\-appscan \-\-list\-apps > /root/apps.txt'" .
.B import
also takes the names from the file it loads.
.TP
.BI show " APP"
One application, with its last 20 changes.
.TP
.BI set " APP " \-\-owner " EMAIL"
Add the application, or change its owner.
.TP
.BI \-\-owner " EMAIL"
With
.B set
or
.BR "site set" :
the owner.
.TP
.BI \-\-component " TEXT"
With
.BR set :
the Jira component (for tickets, when they are on).
.TP
.BI rm " APP"
Remove the application, its watchers and covers.
.TP
.BI "watcher add|rm" " APP EMAIL..."
Add or remove watchers (Cc on the report).
.TP
.BI "covers add|rm" " APP HOST..."
Record or remove production hosts this web app stands in for.
.TP
.BI "site set" " DOMAIN " \-\-owner " EMAIL"
Who should set up a scan for a live website no web app covers;
.BI "site rm" " DOMAIN"
removes it.
.TP
.BI "domain allow" " DOMAIN... " \fR[\fB\-\-note " TEXT" \fR]
Allow owners and watchers at these exact domains (a subdomain is a different
domain).
.BI "domain rm" " DOMAIN..."
removes them;
.B domain list
lists them. With
.I owners.db
in use this list, not
.BR OWNER_ALLOW_DOMAINS ,
is the owner lane's allowlist.
.TP
.BI \-\-note " TEXT"
With
.BR "domain allow" :
why, for the history.
.TP
.B check
What would stop a report reaching someone: no allowed domain, an owner or
watcher at a domain not allowed, a snapshot that does not match the
database. Then notes: applications kept only for covers, allowed domains
nobody uses,
.I app\-owners.txt
still present.
.TP
.BI history " \fR[\fIN\fR]"
The last
.I N
changes (default 30).
.TP
.B export
Print the database as an
.IR app\-owners.txt ,
the way back to the file and a backup you can diff.
.TP
.B snapshot
Rewrite
.I owners.json
from the database, after a restore or a failed write.
.TP
.BI backup " DEST"
Copy the database to
.I DEST
(0600) with SQLite's backup API. An absolute path in a root-controlled
directory; created exclusively, without following a link, and never
overwritten.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FLEET_GROUP
The group given read access to the snapshot; set by the wrapper. Without
it, or as non-root, the snapshot is 0600.
.TP
.B SUDO_USER
Recorded as who made each change.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/owners.db
The database, root:root 0600.
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/owners.json
The snapshot the lanes read, root:ctiagent 0640.
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/app\-owners.txt
Read by the lanes only while there is no snapshot.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done; for
.BR check ,
nothing stops a send.
.TP
.B 1
Refused or failed, with the reason on stderr and nothing written; for
.BR check ,
something stops a send.
.TP
.B 2
Usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-owners init
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-owners import /etc/cti\-agent/app\-owners.txt \e
     \-\-allow\-domains example.com,devshop.example
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-owners check
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-owners set id:123456 \-\-owner lead@example.com
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-owners watcher add id:123456 qa@example.com
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-owners domain allow partner.example \-\-note "agency that builds the shop"
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-owners backup /root/owners\-$(date +%F).db
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-appowners (1),
.BR cti\-mailer (1),
.BR cti\-appscan (1),
.BR fleet\-db (1)
